2026-09-30 21:17:10,757 src.graph.nodes INFO Agent input: {'messages': [HumanMessage(content="# Research Topic\n\nResearch Plan for Strongest Open-Weight Language Models Under 10B Parameters Released in 2025\n\n# Completed Research Steps\n\n## Completed Step 1: Identify and List Open-Weight Language Models Under 10B Parameters Released in 2025\n\n<finding>\n# Research Topic  \n## Identify and List Open-Weight Language Models Under 10B Parameters Released in 2025  \n\n---\n\n### **Problem Statement**  \nIdentify and list publicly available, open-weight language models with fewer than 10 billion parameters that were officially released in 2025. Collect model names, developers, release dates, and official documentation or repositories. Prioritize models with active community engagement, public evaluations, or official announcements.\n\n---\n\n### **Research Findings**\n\n#### **1. DeepSeek**  \nDeepSeek has released open-weight language models under free and open-source software licenses since January 2025. While the exact model parameters are not specified in the provided results, DeepSeek's models are described as open-weight, meaning the parameters are openly shared. The release began in January 2025, and the models are available under open-source licenses.  \n- **Model Name**: DeepSeek (specific variants not detailed)  \n- **Developer**: DeepSeek  \n- **Release Date**: January 2025  \n- **Documentation/Repository**: [DeepSeek - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeepSeek)  \n- **Notes**: Open-weight models with shared parameters; training data not publicly licensed.  \n\n#### **2. OpenAI's gpt-oss Series**  \nThe OpenAI gpt-oss series includes open-weight models such as gpt-oss-120b and gpt-oss-20b. However, these models exceed the 10B parameter threshold (120B and 20B parameters respectively), so they do not meet the requirement of being under 10 billion parameters.  \n- **Model Name**: gpt-oss-120b, gpt-oss-20b  \n- **Developer**: OpenAI  \n- **Release Date**: Not specified in results  \n- **Documentation/Repository**: [gpt-oss on GitHub](https://github.com/openai/gpt-oss)  \n- **Notes**: Exceeds 10B parameter threshold; not relevant to this research scope.  \n\n#### **3. GLM-5**  \nGLM-5 is a large language model with 745 billion parameters, which is significantly above the 10B parameter threshold. It is not relevant to the research scope.  \n- **Model Name**: GLM-5  \n- **Developer**: Zhipu AI  \n- **Parameters**: 745 billion  \n- **Documentation/Repository**: [GLM-5 - Zhipu AI](https://glm5.net/)  \n- **Notes**: Far exceeds the 10B parameter limit; excluded.  \n\n#### **4. Open-LLM Leaderboard (Public Benchmark)**  \nThe Open LLM Leaderboard tracks models under 10 billion parameters, including official model cards, technical reports, and verified third-party evaluations. It serves as a public benchmark for evaluating performance and community engagement.  \n- **Platform**: Open LLM Leaderboard (Hugging Face)  \n- **URL**: [Open LLM Leaderboard](https://huggingface.co/open-llm-leaderboard)  \n- **Notes**: Provides a curated list of models under 10B parameters, with benchmark scores and community engagement metrics. While it does not list specific 2025-released models, it is a key resource for identifying and validating models in this category.  \n\n#### **5. Small Language Model Leaderboard**  \nA dedicated leaderboard ranks models with 10 billion or fewer parameters (or effective parameters for MoE models). Scores are derived from official model cards, technical reports, or third-party evaluations.  \n- **Platform**: Small Language Model Leaderboard  \n- **URL**: [Small Language Model Leaderboard](https://awesomeagents.ai/leaderboards/small-language-model-leaderboard/)  \n- **Notes**: A specialized resource for models under 10B parameters; includes performance metrics and community rankings.  \n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**  \nAfter a comprehensive search, the only model that meets the criteria of being open-weight, under 10 billion parameters, and officially released in 2025 is **DeepSeek**, with its release beginning in January 2025. The DeepSeek models are openly shared under free and open-source software licenses, though training data is not publicly licensed.  \n\nNo other publicly available open-weight language models under 10 billion parameters were found to have been officially released in 2025. The Open LLM Leaderboard and Small Language Model Leaderboard provide valuable resources for identifying such models, but do not list specific 2025-released models.  \n\nThus, the only confirmed model in this category is DeepSeek, with its release starting in January 2025.\n\n---\n\n### **References**  \n- [DeepSeek - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeepSeek)  \n\n- [Open LLM Leaderboard on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/open-llm-leaderboard)  \n\n- [Small Language Model Leaderboard](https://awesomeagents.ai/leaderboards/small-language-model-leaderboard/)  \n\n- [gpt-oss on GitHub](https://github.com/openai/gpt-oss)  \n\n- [GLM-5 - Zhipu AI](https://glm5.net/)\n</finding>\n\n# Current Step\n\n## Title\n\nGather Benchmark Performance Data for Reasoning Capabilities\n\n## Description\n\nCollect detailed results from public reasoning benchmarks such as MATH, GSM8K, HumanEval, or HumanEval-2025 for the top models identified in Step 1. Include scores, comparison metrics, and sources (e.g., papers, GitHub, model websites) to assess relative performance in logical and code reasoning tasks.\n\n## Locale\n\nen-US", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), HumanMessage(content='IMPORTANT: DO NOT include inline citations in the text. Instead, track all sources and include a References section at the end using link reference format. Include an empty line between each citation for better readability. Use this format for each reference:\n- [Source Title](URL)\n\n- [Another Source](URL)', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, name='system')]}
